<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keikavus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · Also known as Kai Kavus ibn Iskandar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keikavus, or Kai Kavus ibn Iskandar, was a Ziyarid prince of the Caspian region in the eleventh century and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus">Qabus ibn Vushmgir</a>. He is known as the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus-nama">Qabus-nama</a>, written in 1082 to 1083 for his son Gilanshah, a Persian book of advice on ethics, statecraft, trade and daily life that is a classic of early Persian prose.</p></div>
